    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. One pass, carrying a little state"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Walk the array once and keep a few variables up to date: the best so far, the cheapest so far, a running sum, a counter. **Signals:** a single answer about the whole array; \"maximum\", \"minimum\", \"best time\"."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock, Majority Element, Maximum Subarray Sum (Kadane) and Increasing Triplet Subsequence — all in **Most asked interview questions**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Two pointers"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Two indexes moving through the array — from both ends toward the middle, or one reading and one writing. **Signals:** a **sorted** array, pairs or triplets, palindromes, \"in place\", \"remove\", \"partition\"."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Move Zeroes, Sort 0s, 1s and 2s, Container With Most Water, 3Sum, and the in-place version of Merge Intervals."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. Sliding window"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A range `[left, right]` that slides along the array, adding the element entering and removing the one leaving, instead of recomputing the whole range. **Signals:** \"contiguous subarray\", \"substring\", \"window of size k\", \"longest / shortest subarray such that…\"."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "A close cousin of Kadane's algorithm in **Maximum Subarray Sum** — and the reason **Subarray Sum Equals K** needs a different tool: with negative numbers, a window can't tell when to shrink."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "4. Prefix sums (and prefix products)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Precompute running totals so that the sum of any range is one subtraction. **Signals:** many range-sum questions, \"subarray sum equals k\", \"everything to my left / right\"."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Product of Array Except Self, and Subarray Sum Equals K."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "5. Hash map or hash set"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Trade memory for speed: remember what you've seen so that \"have I seen x?\" costs O(1). **Signals:** \"pair that sums to target\", \"duplicates\", \"frequency\", \"first unique\", unsorted input where sorting would lose information (like original indexes)."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Two Sum, Subarray Sum Equals K, and the hash-set versions of Find All Duplicates and 3Sum."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "6. Sort first"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Sorting costs O(n log n), but afterwards equal values sit together, intervals line up by start, and the smallest and largest are at the ends. **Signals:** intervals and meetings, grouping, \"closest\", when the original order doesn't matter."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Merge Intervals and 3Sum — and Kth Largest Element, where sorting is the brute force that a heap or quickselect beats."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "7. Binary search"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Halve the search space each step. Works on sorted values — and, more surprisingly, on any **yes/no question that flips once** (\"can we finish with capacity c?\"). **Signals:** sorted input, \"O(log n)\", \"minimum capacity / speed / time such that…\"."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Find Peak Element — binary search on the slope of the values, without them being sorted."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "8. Index tricks: the array as its own memory"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "When values are in the range `1..n`, a value can point at an index: place each number at its \"home\" slot, or mark slots by flipping signs. **Signals:** \"numbers from 1 to n\", \"missing\", \"duplicate\", \"O(1) extra space\"."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Find All Duplicates in an Array — marking each value as seen by flipping the sign at its index."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "How to use this map"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "When you read a new problem: write the brute force first, then scan this list and ask \"which of these removes the repeated work?\" If two nested loops are searching for something, a hash map or two pointers usually removes the inner loop. If you keep re-adding the same range, it's a sliding window or prefix sums. If the input is sorted and you're not using that, you're probably missing two pointers or binary search."
    }
  ],
